@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+# ECON-02B2d16 — Dependency-ordered NPC industrial-line retirement
+
+**Project:** Single Galaxy — original game, separate from EVEmu.  
+**Date:** 2026-11-01.  
+**Exact baseline:** `SingleGalaxy_ECON02B2d15_Production_Shutdown_Inventory_Evacuation_Source_Tests_2026-11-01.zip`.  
+**Status:** Linux implementation and complete native regression verified. Not committed to GitHub. Windows GUI acceptance still open.
+
+## Outcome
+
+The colony administration now exposes one explicit **Retire line with NPC crew** authorization and a **Cancel pending retirement** command. Once approved, one individually identified on-site resident coordinates existing authoritative shutdown, physical goods evacuation, workshop-connected conveyor removal and two-stage machine dismantling. The system uses the same native C++ `PersistentWorld`, physical module ledger, 20-kg NPC workshop-goods custody and SQLite NPC job tables introduced in d13–d15. No free inventory, instant demolition, or independent browser writer has been added.
+
+### Ordered lifecycle
+
+1. **Stop** new production with `SetWorkshopShutdown`. The command is committed by the authoritative simulation; the SQL stage advances by observing `shutdown_requested`, including after a crash between the world and SQL updates.
+2. **Drain** outstanding workshop work-in-progress and release named operators using the d15 scheduler. A missing or revoked shutdown blocks further work.
+3. **Evacuate** real workshop aluminum, panels, frames and scrap with the d15 bounded NPC cargo workflow. A worker physically walks from installed workshop to the **original built storage bin**, and each pickup/delivery has an audited authoritative world receipt. A full bin or blocked route leaves cargo in custody; the coordinator waits rather than teleporting it.
+4. **Unbuild conveyors** attached to `kWorkshopConveyorNode`, smallest link ID first. Each real segment and link requires a durable named-person d13 dismantling job and returns a module in worker cargo; the next step starts only after cargo reaches the depot. Unrelated colony links are untouched.
+5. **Remove the finisher**, after its WIP, operators and goods are cleared, through the d14 durable NPC machine job.
+6. **Remove the primary processor**, last. The job returns its recovered construction module via the same physical depot route. A completed line has no installed workshop or attached links, and its retirement record becomes inactive.
+
+SQLite table `sg_line_retirements` stores per-site authorization, resident identity, phase, active/cancellation flags, last processed world tick, and human-readable reason. It does **not** duplicate physical inventory. The simulation snapshot format and resource ledger remain unchanged in d16 because the coordinator introduces no new world action or physical asset class. The existing world evidence is authoritative across restarts.
+
+## Access and cancellation
+
+- Only the current owner/ship/site session can approve or cancel retirement. The relay enforces local origin, token, typed body, and site validation; the native `Control` validates ownership, location and resident identity.
+- The resident must not already be building, hauling, evacuating or dismantling, and must not carry other physical cargo when retirement is assigned. Duplicate active per-site retirement requests are rejected.
+- While retirement is active, the native control API rejects competing manual workshop resume, evacuation, conveyor teardown and machine teardown requests. The coordinator calls only the existing guarded native crew methods.
+- Cancellation stops scheduling further irreversible steps, marks in-flight child jobs for safe return, waits for carried cargo to be physically returned, and leaves already completed dismantling and shutdown unchanged. It **does not** restart production or rebuild destroyed components. A partially retired workshop can be expressly reauthorized if it still exists.
+- Errors/shortages are visible as reasons in the existing colony crew panel. In particular, no secondary warehouse or impossible path is silently substituted.
+
+## Local validation
+
+| Check | Verified result |
+| --- | --- |
+| Full native CMake/Ninja build | PASS |
+| CTest tests 1–72 | 72/72 PASS |
+| CTest tests 73–107 | 35/35 PASS |
+| Native d16 retirement simulation | PASS |
+| Authenticated d16 HTTP with mid-cargo process restart | PASS |
+| Authorization: forged token, foreign origin, wrong site, malformed NPC | REJECTED |
+| Repeat authorization; manual production resume during active retirement | REJECTED |
+| NPC cancellation during *actual cargo transit*, restart, physical cargo return | PASS |
+| World material conservation through evacuation, link removal and both machines | PASS |
+| JavaScript tests | 93/93 PASS |
+| Offline Chromium colony crew controls (including authorize/cancel) | PASS |
+| Offline Chromium structure controls | PASS |
+
+The native 107 tests were run in complementary groups after a clean full build, not one uninterrupted run. UI browser smoke uses a mocked browser transport; the separate Python HTTP case exercises the real relay, token controls, C++ game host and SQLite save.
+
+## Remaining boundaries
+
+1. **Current line scope is the original two-stage workshop**. An arbitrary multi-machine production graph, topological dependency planner for generic machines, and synchronized crews are not implemented. The finisher-before-primary order is explicit for the currently modeled line.
+2. **Inventory evacuation currently targets only the original built local bin.** Selecting among several extra storage nodes, outpost-scale inventory scheduling and distributed goods transport require additional authoritative custody commands, capacity checks and NPC delivery path tests. These are open gates, not implied by this d16 implementation.
+3. This is a *local 2D grid* NPC scheduler, not full 3D navigation or animated person/cargo transport.
+4. The retirement coordinator runs when the colony simulation runs; it does not promise asynchronous server execution while the game executable is closed. Existing persistent jobs resume when it starts again.
+5. The native `Control` prevents concurrent *API* modifications to the same line. Direct core commands outside this host boundary are not yet covered by a universal world-level retirement lock; a subsequent central authorization barrier should close that gap.
+6. Windows compilation, installer execution and independent graphical acceptance are not verified in this Linux milestone.
+7. GitHub is the source repository and milestone archive, not a development hub. This work has **not been pushed**.
+
+## Recommended next gate
+
+**ECON-02B2d17 — Generic factory retirement planning and multi-destination goods evacuation**, introducing a graph-derived teardown order, physical storage selection/capacity, per-leg NPC custody and a central world-level retirement lock. Test conflicting concurrent line requests, blocked routes, full bins, resumed NPC work and save schema compatibility before extending this beyond the existing two-stage workshop.
